  "cards": [
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.030.c1",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Chapter 3 finale. The last ancient objection falls.",
      "body": "Back in Level 23, the strongest case for a still Earth was simple: the stars never shift. For about 2,000 years, nobody could see them move. Then someone did."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.030.c2",
      "type": "timeline",
      "headline": "How we figured it out",
      "events": [
        {
          "when": "~240 BCE",
          "label": "Eratosthenes measures Earth with the noon Sun"
        },
        {
          "when": "~150 CE",
          "label": "Ptolemy puts a still Earth at the center"
        },
        {
          "when": "1543",
          "label": "Copernicus hands the center to the Sun"
        },
        {
          "when": "1610",
          "label": "Galileo finds moons circling Jupiter"
        },
        {
          "when": "1687",
          "label": "Newton's gravity explains why planets move"
        },
        {
          "when": "1838",
          "label": "Bessel measures stellar parallax"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.030.c3",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "In 1838 Friedrich Bessel finally measured a star shifting as Earth orbits.",
      "context": "The star, 61 Cygni, moved by a tiny angle over the year. That shift, parallax, is exactly what the doubters of Level 23 had looked for and never found. It gave the first measured distance to a star, and direct proof that Earth goes around the Sun, just as Copernicus argued. The ground still feels still. It just isn't."
    },
